  "slug": "microbe-comprehensive-mapping-583d",
  "summary": "New releases of MGnify, BGC Atlas, and Unipept, plus sample-specific databases and organellar screening, expand reference-driven microbiome annotation.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is reference resources and database-driven tooling for annotating microbial community data across sequencing and proteomics. BGC Atlas v2 holds over 16 million predicted biosynthetic gene clusters searchable by taxonomic, environmental and geographic context. MGnify v6 adds amplicon variant analysis, expanded viral and mobilome annotation, Nextflow/Prefect pipeline automation and improved programmatic access. On the metaproteomics side, Unipept now supports multi-sample projects with taxonomic and pathway comparison, confidence scoring and a faster, lower-memory API. NovoTax instead builds sample-specific prokaryotic proteome databases directly from bottom-up MS data via de novo sequencing, reducing dependence on generic references. Identification resolution is the focus of RADSuite, which pairs a curated 16S copy database (RADlib) with V-region detection (RADx) for species-level calls, and of metaEukaryome, which uses mitochondrial and plastid sequences to recover eukaryotes from 75,727 metagenomes, revealing cross-domain co-occurrence.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
